# shir_nodes/reg_sub.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Regex substitution (lowered from `sed 's/pattern/repl/'`).
#
# Backends: Perl s///, JS replace(), C regex, Rust regex::replace
# Fallback: sh2.regSub(text, pattern, replacement, global)

node RegSub
tag "RegSub"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field pattern: string
field replacement: string
field global: bool
field line_mode: bool
# Spec: TextOps#reg_sub_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "RegSub: the substitution engine, non-global and global"). Stated over an ABSTRACT
# matcher (leftmost match reported as a (prefix, matched, suffix) decomposition, or
# None) plus an ABSTRACT replacement R of the matched text, which is where sed's
# replacement language lives. Proved, for an arbitrary matcher and replacement:
# non-global is the one-step fold (unmatched prefix, R of the match, suffix untouched)
# and rs_all at fuel 1 is exactly it; global is the fuel-indexed fold over CONSECUTIVE
# decompositions of the successive suffixes, with fuel 0 the identity; an unmatched
# subject comes back byte-identical at any fuel, both arms; if the matcher reports
# genuine decompositions and R is the identity the whole global fold is the identity
# (the engine invents and loses nothing); and for a subject with no LF — exactly the
# class the lowering admits — the per-line fold equals the whole-subject substitution.
#
# TWO TREE FACTS the spec is shaped around, both stated rather than assumed: the
# lowering builds this node only for a provably SINGLE-LINE literal
# (transforms/text_ops.rs, `single_line_literal`) because a whole-string application
# would otherwise match across newlines; and `line_mode` is declared here and set by
# the proposed lowering in the backend worktrees, but no renderer in this tree reads
# it, so the per-line arm is modelled at the abstraction level and is not claimed to be
# what any backend emits today.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): greediness, alternation preference and search
# order (the matcher hands back the leftmost match, and an engine that chose another is
# not caught); the regex DIALECT (ERE/RE2 classes, anchors, quantifiers, groups), case
# folding, locale and Unicode rules; the replacement SYNTAX itself (`&`, \N, \&, \n)
# — R abstracts it, with `expand` in the Substitute theory as the concrete expansion;
# the EMPTY-MATCH advance rule, which belongs to the engine's own find() after a match;
# the source's TRAILING NEWLINE (the lowering's trailing_newline guard owns that); and
# argument evaluation order, side effects, and anything beyond bytes.
spec: TextOps#reg_sub_sound
